Here's a JavaScript function that switches the order of adjectives in a sentence:

```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  // Split the sentence into an array of words
  const words = sentence.split(" ");

  // Find the index of the first adjective (word that ends with 'e')
  let firstAdjIndex = -1;
  for (let I = 0; I < words.length; i++) {
    if (words[i].endsWith("e") && !words[i - 1].endsWith("e")) {
      firstAdjIndex = i;
      break;
    }
  }

  // If we couldn't find any adjectives, return the original sentence
  if (firstAdjIndex === -1) return sentence;

  // Swap the first and second adjectives (if there's a second one)
  const secondAdjIndex = firstAdjIndex - 1;
  [words[secondAdjIndex], words[firstAdjIndex]] = [words[firstAdjIndex], words[secondAdjIndex]];

  // Join the words back into a sentence
  return words.join(" ");
}
```

This function works by iterating through the words in the sentence, looking for words that end with 'e'. If it finds such a word, it assumes that it's an adjective. It then keeps track of the indices of the first and second adjectives (if they exist). After finding all the adjectives, it swaps the first and second ones, and returns the modified sentence. Note that this implementation assumes that all adjectives will come before nouns, which is true in English but not necessarily in other languages. If your input sentences might contain adjectives after nouns, you may need to modify the logic. Also, note that this function doesn't handle cases where there are more than two adjectives, as it only swaps the first and second ones. You could extend it to swap more than two adjectives, but that would make the algorithm slower and more complex. Here's an example usage of the function:

