```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    char *input_str = "";
    int i;

    // Concatenate all command-line arguments into a single string
    for (i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
        input_str = realloc(input_str, strlen(input_str) + strlen(argv[i]) + 1);
        strcat(input_str, argv[i]);
        strcat(input_str, " ");
    }

    // Remove trailing space character
    input_str[strlen(input_str) - 1] = '\0';

    printf("Converted command-line arguments: %s\n", input_str);

    return 0;
}
```

In this program, we first initialize an empty string called `input_str`. We then iterate through each argument in `argv`, using `realloc()` to dynamically allocate memory for the concatenated string and `strcat()` to join the current argument with the existing string. We also append a space character between arguments for readability. After processing all arguments, we remove the trailing space character and print the resulting string. Note that this implementation assumes that the total size of the concatenated string won't exceed the maximum value representable by a signed integer (`INT_MAX`). If you expect very large inputs, you may need to modify this approach or use a different data structure for storing the concatenated string.

