Here's an implementation of the requested decorator using NestJS:

```typescript
import { Injectable } from '@nestjs/common';
import { ExecutionContext, NestMiddleware } from '@nestjs/core';

@Injectable()
export class DevModeMiddleware implements NestMiddleware {
  use(context: ExecutionContext, next: () => any) {
    const req = context.switchToHttp().getRequest();

    if (req.app.get('env') === 'development') {
      return next();
    } else {
      return false;
    }
  }
}

// Example usage:
@UseGuards(AuthGuard)
@UsePipes(ValidationPipe)
@UseInterceptors(ResponseInterceptor)
@DevModeMiddleware()
export class MyController {}
```

In this example, we create a new middleware called `DevModeMiddleware`. It takes an `ExecutionContext` object and a `next` callback function as parameters.

We then extract the request object from the `ExecutionContext`, get the application environment from the request's app property, and compare it with the "development" value. If they match, we call the `next` function to continue with the execution chain. Otherwise, we simply return `false`.

This decorator can then be applied to controllers or routes that need special handling during development mode. The `DevModeMiddleware` will only allow requests during development mode to pass through, while blocking them in production mode.

Note: You may also want to add some logging or error handling to better inform developers during dev mode. This is not included in this simple example, but you can easily extend the `use` method to do so.

